  "analysis": "Kept R1 model (C2: 25 inclusion @ default scale) shows the miss pattern is now dominated by SMALL instances: inclusion misses median 34px vs 90px train (146/179 val inclusions in low tertile, recall 0.34); scratches median miss 40px; patches 52px. R1 evidence: allocation to inclusion was the dominant lever (+0.20 AP50), small scale (0.3-0.8) gave scratches a large gain (0.746 vs 0.631) and a modest inclusion gain, but hurt patches (0.735 vs 0.791). So R2 tests a ladder: (1) heavy inclusion + small inclusion scale; (2) same but also small scale for scratches (per-class scale from R1 evidence, patches stay default); (3) same as 2 but inclusion sources restricted to thin vertical streaks matching the missed inclusions (excluding fragment clusters 11/150 and broad multi-streak 99). All 30/10/10 to keep headroom for R3.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hypothesis": "Combination hypothesis: R1 showed allocation to inclusion dominates (C2 +0.14 mAP) and small scale helped inclusion modestly (C1 0.292 vs C4 0.258). Combining both - 30 inclusion pastes at 0.3-0.8x targeting the 34px missed median - should push inclusion AP50 above 0.50 and mAP50 above 0.65 without dropping patches/scratches below ~0.70. Confirmed if it beats the control (kept plan) on mAP50 with the gain concentrated in inclusion.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 30, "patches": 10, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.8, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Per-class scale hypothesis: R1 showed small scale gave scratches a large gain (0.746 vs 0.631 at identical allocation) while hurting patches (0.735 vs 0.791). So apply small scale only where it worked: inclusion AND scratches at 0.3-0.8x, patches at default. Confirmed if it beats candidate 1 on mAP50 with scratches AP50 > 0.74 and patches holding ~0.74.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 30, "patches": 10, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.8, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.8, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Source-matching hypothesis: the missed inclusions are thin, low-contrast vertical streaks (median 34px), but uniform source selection includes fragment clusters (11, 150) and a broad multi-streak (99) that don't match that appearance. Restricting inclusion sources to thin streaks (100, 106, 131, 268, 277, 297, 50) at small scale should raise inclusion AP50, especially low-tertile recall, over candidate 2. Confirmed if inclusion AP50 exceeds candidate 2's while scratches/patches are comparable.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 30, "patches": 10, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.8, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": [100, 106, 131, 268, 277, 297, 50]},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.8, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    }
  ]
}

